Object.assign(window.VIDEO_DETAILS, {"5eqRuVp65eY": {"summary": "Welch Labs shows that when you train a model its error falls fast and then flattens, and bigger models reach lower error at higher compute. Plotted on log axes, a whole family of training curves bumps against a boundary no model crosses, the compute-efficient frontier. The video walks through the OpenAI 2020 scaling papers, GPT-3, DeepMind's Chinchilla result and a theory linking scaling to the dimension of the data manifold. It ends on the open question of whether this is a law of nature for intelligence or an artefact of today's neural-network approach.", "keyPoints": ["Training error drops quickly, then levels off; larger models reach lower error but need more compute.", "On log-log axes, training runs line up against a frontier no model crosses.", "Error follows power laws in compute, model size and dataset size: the three neural scaling laws.", "The trends hold across architectures as long as reasonable choices are made.", "Chinchilla showed many models were undertrained and that data and parameters should be scaled together.", "One theory ties the slope of scaling to the intrinsic dimension of the data manifold.", "Whether scaling laws are fundamental or method-specific remains unresolved."], "whyBest": ["Beautiful, accurate animations turn log-log plots into something intuitive.", "Cites the primary papers (Kaplan 2020, Chinchilla, GPT-4 report) rather than hype.", "Explains why the laws work, not just that they exist."], "learn": ["What a scaling law is and how to read a log-log plot", "The difference between compute, parameter and data scaling", "Why Chinchilla changed how labs size models", "Theories for why scaling laws exist", "Why extrapolating them is still an open bet"], "audience": "Anyone curious why AI labs keep building bigger models, from students to practitioners.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Pause at each log-log chart and trace the frontier line with your finger; the whole argument lives there.", "terms": [{"term": "Scaling law", "def": "An empirical power-law formula predicting a model's loss from its compute, size or data."}, {"term": "Compute-efficient frontier", "def": "The lowest error achievable for a given compute budget; no training run crosses it."}, {"term": "Chinchilla scaling", "def": "DeepMind's 2022 finding that parameters and training tokens should grow together for compute-optimal training."}, {"term": "Irreducible loss", "def": "The error floor a model cannot go below, reflecting inherent unpredictability in the data."}], "oneLine": "Why AI error follows a straight line on log plots, and what it might mean."}, "aR20FWCCjAs": {"summary": "OpenAI co-founder and SSI chief Ilya Sutskever tells Dwarkesh Patel that 2012 to 2020 was an age of research, 2020 to 2025 an age of scaling, and that we are now back to research, just with big computers. He argues pre-training data is clearly finite and that simply making things 100x bigger will not transform results on its own. The conversation covers why models are 'jagged', why humans generalise far better from much less data, emotions as value functions, and SSI's strategy. It became the reference point for the 2025-26 'is scaling hitting a wall' debate.", "keyPoints": ["Sutskever frames AI history as research era, then scaling era, now research again.", "Pre-training data is finite, so the classic recipe must change.", "He doubts that 100x more scale alone would transform capabilities.", "Models are jagged: strong on evals, surprisingly weak at generalising in practice.", "Humans learn far more deeply from far less data than current models.", "He suggests value functions, akin to emotions, matter for learning efficiently.", "Discusses SSI's approach and how to make AGI go well."], "whyBest": ["Comes straight from one of the people who launched the scaling era.", "Gives a concrete thesis (data limits, generalisation) rather than vague doubt.", "Pairs well with optimistic takes for a balanced view."], "learn": ["The 'age of scaling' vs 'age of research' framing", "Why the data wall matters for pre-training", "What model 'jaggedness' means", "Why generalisation is the key open problem", "How a frontier researcher thinks about what comes next"], "audience": "Followers of AI progress who want the strongest case that scaling alone is not enough.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Watch the first 30 minutes (jaggedness and 'what are we scaling?') if you are short on time.", "terms": [{"term": "Pre-training", "def": "The first, large-scale phase of training where a model learns to predict text from huge datasets."}, {"term": "Generalisation", "def": "A model's ability to perform well on situations unlike its training data."}, {"term": "Jaggedness", "def": "Uneven capability: excelling at some hard tasks while failing at seemingly easy ones."}, {"term": "Value function", "def": "In RL, an estimate of how good a situation is; Sutskever links it to emotions in humans."}], "oneLine": "Ilya: scaling's era is ending; research and generalisation come next."}, "dDUC-LqVrPU": {"summary": "Dr Mike Pound of the University of Nottingham walks through a paper titled 'No Zero-Shot Without Exponential Data'. It suggests that performance on a concept improves only with exponentially more training examples of that concept. That implies diminishing returns from simply making generative models and datasets larger, especially for rare concepts. It is a calm, whiteboard-style counterweight to claims that scaling will keep delivering forever.", "keyPoints": ["Looks at the paper 'No Zero-Shot Without Exponential Data'.", "Model performance on a concept tracks how often that concept appears in training data.", "Getting linear gains appears to need exponentially more data.", "Rare concepts in the 'long tail' are where models struggle most.", "This implies diminishing returns from bigger models trained the same way.", "Suggests new ideas, not just more data, may be needed."], "whyBest": ["Grounded in a specific paper rather than opinion.", "Short and accessible, with whiteboard sketches.", "A useful sceptical counterpoint to scaling optimism."], "learn": ["What 'zero-shot' performance means", "How training-data frequency shapes model ability", "Why exponential data needs imply a ceiling", "The long-tail problem in AI", "How to read scaling claims critically"], "audience": "Beginners and students wanting a balanced, sceptical perspective on scaling.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch it after the Welch Labs video so the log-scale intuition is fresh.", "terms": [{"term": "Zero-shot", "def": "Doing a task without having been given examples of it."}, {"term": "Diminishing returns", "def": "Each extra unit of input (data, compute) yields a smaller improvement."}, {"term": "Long tail", "def": "The many rare concepts that each appear only a few times in training data."}, {"term": "Log-linear trend", "def": "A pattern where linear gains need exponential increases in input."}], "oneLine": "Do AI gains need exponentially more data? A paper says maybe."}, "GrloGdp5wdc": {"summary": "In this clip from the Lex Fridman Podcast, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei explains the scaling hypothesis: bigger networks, more data and more compute keep producing better models. He describes how he came to believe in it from his own research history and why the trend has held across many orders of magnitude. He also discusses what could stop it, such as running out of data or compute, and why he still expects progress to continue. It is a short, accessible insider view of the bet the whole industry has made.", "keyPoints": ["Dario lays out the scaling hypothesis: scale network size, data and compute together.", "He traces his belief in scaling back to his early research experience.", "Scaling has held across many orders of magnitude so far.", "Ingredients need to be scaled in proportion, like reagents in a reaction.", "Possible limits include data scarcity and compute cost.", "He explains why he still expects scaling to keep working."], "whyBest": ["First-hand view from the CEO of a frontier lab.", "Short clip, so it is easy to fit in.", "A clear optimistic counterpart to the sceptical videos in this topic."], "learn": ["The scaling hypothesis in a lab leader's words", "Why labs keep investing in bigger training runs", "What might break the trend", "How an insider weighs data and compute limits"], "audience": "General audiences who want the optimistic case without maths.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Contrast what Dario says here with Ilya Sutskever's 2025 'age of research' interview.", "terms": [{"term": "Scaling hypothesis", "def": "The bet that capability keeps rising predictably as models, data and compute grow."}, {"term": "Orders of magnitude", "def": "Factors of ten; scaling trends are tracked across many of them."}, {"term": "Synthetic data", "def": "Training data generated by models themselves, proposed as one answer to data limits."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic's CEO on why bigger AI keeps getting better."}, "21EYKqUsPfg": {"summary": "Richard Sutton, the father of reinforcement learning, 2024 Turing Award winner and author of the essay 'The Bitter Lesson', tells Dwarkesh Patel that LLMs are a dead end. Dwarkesh's steel-man of the view is that LLMs cannot learn on the job, so no matter how much we scale, a new architecture enabling continual learning will be needed. They debate whether LLMs really embody the bitter lesson, since they rely heavily on human-generated data. The episode ends with whether the bitter lesson will still apply after AGI.", "keyPoints": ["Sutton wrote 'The Bitter Lesson': general methods that leverage compute beat hand-built human knowledge.", "He argues LLMs are not truly bitter-lesson-pilled because they depend on human data.", "LLMs cannot learn continually from experience on the job.", "Sutton favours agents that learn from their own experience and rewards.", "Scaling LLMs may hit the limits of available human data.", "They discuss whether the bitter lesson still holds after AGI."], "whyBest": ["Hear the bitter lesson's author interpret it himself.", "A sharp, contrarian view from a foundational researcher.", "Dwarkesh pushes back, so both sides get aired."], "learn": ["What 'The Bitter Lesson' actually says", "Why some see LLMs as against its spirit", "The case for continual learning from experience", "How RL thinking differs from LLM thinking", "Limits of human-data-driven scaling"], "audience": "Intermediate learners who know what LLMs and RL are and want a deep debate.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Read Sutton's short 2019 essay 'The Bitter Lesson' first; it takes five minutes.", "terms": [{"term": "The Bitter Lesson", "def": "Sutton's 2019 essay: methods that scale with compute ultimately beat human-designed knowledge."}, {"term": "Reinforcement learning", "def": "Learning by trial and error from rewards rather than from labelled examples."}, {"term": "Continual learning", "def": "A system's ability to keep learning from new experience after deployment."}, {"term": "Experience", "def": "In Sutton's view, data an agent generates by acting in the world, not human text."}], "oneLine": "The Bitter Lesson's author says LLMs are a dead end."}, "iO844izo9kw": {"summary": "AI Explained lays out the narratives and counter-narratives circulating in late 2025, then checks the facts behind each. It contrasts Anthropic's CEO saying the industry knows how to scale to AGI with Ilya Sutskever's view that we are back in an age of research. It also covers job-apocalypse headlines, recursive self-improvement, a paper on forecasting progress under a compute slowdown, and new models such as Gemini 3 Deep Think, Claude Opus 4.5, DeepSeek and Mistral Large 3. The goal is to let viewers decide for themselves rather than pick a side for them.", "keyPoints": ["Late-2025 AI news is full of directly contradictory claims.", "Dario Amodei says scaling will reach AGI; Ilya Sutskever says research is needed again.", "Job-apocalypse headlines are weighed against usage data.", "Covers whether recursive self-improvement is needed for big gains.", "Discusses what a compute slowdown would mean for METR-style trends.", "Compares the newest frontier models of the moment."], "whyBest": ["Balanced and evidence-led, citing sources for every claim.", "Puts the scaling debate in its real 2025 context.", "Good for understanding why experts disagree."], "learn": ["The main competing narratives about AI progress", "How to weigh claims about scaling to AGI", "Why compute growth matters for forecasts", "How to check headlines against data"], "audience": "Viewers who follow AI news and want a level-headed synthesis.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Keep the description open; the linked sources are as useful as the video.", "terms": [{"term": "AGI", "def": "Artificial general intelligence: AI matching humans across most cognitive tasks."}, {"term": "Recursive self-improvement", "def": "AI systems improving the AI research that builds them, possibly accelerating progress."}, {"term": "Compute slowdown", "def": "A scenario where growth in training compute slows, potentially slowing capability gains."}], "oneLine": "Two sides of every big AI claim, with the facts."}, "s7_NlkBwdj8": {"summary": "Keras creator and ARC-AGI author François Chollet argues in his AGI-24 keynote that scaling LLMs will not get us to AGI. He shows failure modes such as models breaking on small rephrasings of memorised problems, suggesting performance tracks task familiarity rather than complexity. He defines intelligence as the process of efficiently acquiring new skills, introduces the ARC-AGI benchmark and his 'kaleidoscope hypothesis' of abstraction. He closes by proposing to combine deep learning with program synthesis.", "keyPoints": ["LLMs often fail on trivial rephrasings of problems they have memorised.", "Performance tracks how familiar a task is, not how complex it is.", "Intelligence is the efficiency of acquiring new skills, not stored skill.", "ARC-AGI is designed to test generalisation to genuinely novel tasks.", "The kaleidoscope hypothesis: the world is built from reusable abstractions.", "Chollet proposes merging deep learning with discrete program search."], "whyBest": ["A rigorous, example-driven critique rather than a vibe.", "Introduces ARC-AGI, a benchmark central to the scaling debate.", "Offers a concrete alternative path, not just criticism."], "learn": ["Why benchmark scores can overstate intelligence", "Chollet's definition of intelligence", "What ARC-AGI measures", "Program synthesis as a complement to deep learning", "The memorisation vs reasoning debate"], "audience": "Intermediate to advanced learners interested in AGI definitions and benchmarks.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Watch with the ARC-AGI website open and try a few puzzles yourself.", "terms": [{"term": "ARC-AGI", "def": "Chollet's benchmark of novel grid puzzles that test generalisation from few examples."}, {"term": "Abstraction", "def": "A reusable concept or pattern that can be applied to new situations."}, {"term": "Program synthesis", "def": "Automatically searching for programs that solve a task."}, {"term": "Skill vs intelligence", "def": "Chollet's distinction between stored abilities and the capacity to gain new ones."}], "oneLine": "Chollet: AGI needs abstraction and generalisation, not just scale."}, "gTlxCrsUcFM": {"summary": "Julian Schrittwieser, an Anthropic researcher who previously worked on AlphaGo Zero and MuZero at Google DeepMind, unpacks his viral post 'Failing to Understand the Exponential, Again'. He argues people underestimate exponential trends, comparing it to early COVID, and points to task lengths that agents can handle doubling every few months. The conversation covers scaling reinforcement learning on top of language models and what AlphaGo's Move 37 suggests about AI finding genuinely novel ideas. It is the optimistic counterpart to 'scaling is hitting a wall'.", "keyPoints": ["Schrittwieser's post argues we keep failing to grasp exponential trends.", "He compares AI progress perception to early COVID case counts.", "Agent task lengths have been doubling every few months.", "RL is becoming a new axis of scaling beyond pre-training.", "AlphaGo's Move 37 is a model for AI producing novel insights.", "Discusses what the next few years may look like if trends hold."], "whyBest": ["Insider view from someone behind AlphaGo Zero and MuZero.", "Connects scaling laws, RL and agent time-horizon data.", "A balanced foil to the sceptical videos in this topic."], "learn": ["Why exponentials are hard to intuit", "How RL scaling differs from pre-training scaling", "How agent time-horizon trends feed forecasts", "Lessons from AlphaGo for LLMs"], "audience": "Intermediate viewers who know the scaling debate and want the bullish case.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Read his blog post 'Failing to Understand the Exponential, Again' alongside it.", "terms": [{"term": "Exponential trend", "def": "Growth where a quantity doubles at a regular interval."}, {"term": "Move 37", "def": "AlphaGo's surprising 2016 move that human experts initially thought was a mistake."}, {"term": "Scaling RL", "def": "Spending ever more compute on reinforcement learning to improve models."}, {"term": "Task horizon", "def": "How long a task, in human time, an AI can complete reliably."}], "oneLine": "An Anthropic researcher says we're still misreading AI's exponential."}, "RveLjcNl0ds": {"summary": "Shaw Talebi explains the technical ideas behind reasoning models trained with large-scale reinforcement learning, OpenAI's o1 and DeepSeek-R1. He covers test-time compute and 'thinking' tokens, then walks through the DeepSeek-R1 paper in detail. That includes R1-Zero's prompt template, its rule-based reward and the GRPO algorithm, followed by R1's four-step pipeline of supervised fine-tuning with chain-of-thought data, R1-Zero-style RL, mixed-data SFT, and final RL with RLHF. Clear chapters make it easy to follow or revisit.", "keyPoints": ["o1 showed performance rises with both more RL training and more thinking at test time.", "Thinking tokens let a model reason step by step before answering.", "DeepSeek-R1-Zero learned reasoning from pure RL with rule-based rewards.", "GRPO scores a group of sampled answers against each other instead of using a critic model.", "R1 adds cold-start SFT on chain-of-thought data to fix readability issues.", "The final pipeline alternates SFT and RL, ending with RL plus RLHF.", "Distilled smaller R1 models make reasoning more accessible."], "whyBest": ["Goes paper-level deep while staying structured and clear.", "Timestamped chapters for every stage of training.", "Covers both the OpenAI and DeepSeek approaches."], "learn": ["What test-time compute is", "How R1-Zero learned to reason with RL", "How GRPO works at a high level", "The full four-stage R1 training pipeline", "How to access and use DeepSeek models"], "audience": "Developers and students with some ML background.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Skip the GRPO technical section on first viewing, then come back to it.", "terms": [{"term": "Test-time compute", "def": "Extra computation a model spends while answering, such as longer reasoning."}, {"term": "GRPO", "def": "Group Relative Policy Optimisation: RL that compares several sampled answers to each other."}, {"term": "R1-Zero", "def": "DeepSeek's model trained with RL directly on the base model, with no supervised warm-up."}, {"term": "Distillation", "def": "Training a smaller model to imitate a larger one's outputs."}, {"term": "Thinking tokens", "def": "Tokens a model generates to reason internally before giving its final answer."}], "oneLine": "How o1 and DeepSeek-R1 learned to think, step by step."}, "kv8frWeKoeo": {"summary": "This short video explains the key insights of the DeepSeek R1 paper in everyday language. It focuses on three ideas: chain-of-thought reasoning, reinforcement learning and model distillation. Using the paper's own graphs, it shows how R1's accuracy rises as RL training continues, and compares that with OpenAI's o1. It is a gentle first step before more technical treatments.", "keyPoints": ["Chain of thought means the model writes out its reasoning step by step.", "Reinforcement learning lets the model improve by trial and error rather than from labelled answers.", "R1's accuracy climbs steadily as RL training continues.", "Comparisons with o1 show R1 catching up through RL.", "Distillation transfers a big model's reasoning into smaller models.", "Smaller distilled models can run on more accessible hardware."], "whyBest": ["Genuinely beginner-friendly analogies.", "Under nine minutes.", "Uses real figures from the paper."], "learn": ["What chain-of-thought reasoning is", "Why RL matters for reasoning models", "What model distillation does", "Why DeepSeek R1 made headlines"], "audience": "Complete beginners and non-technical viewers.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch this first, then move on to the Shaw Talebi video for depth.", "terms": [{"term": "Chain of thought", "def": "Step-by-step written reasoning a model produces before its answer."}, {"term": "Reinforcement learning", "def": "Learning by trying things and being rewarded for good outcomes."}, {"term": "Model distillation", "def": "Teaching a small 'student' model to copy a large 'teacher' model."}], "oneLine": "DeepSeek R1's big ideas, explained simply in under nine minutes."}, "r7wCtN3mzXk": {"summary": "Matthew Berman walks through Anthropic's paper 'Reasoning Models Don't Always Say What They Think'. Researchers slipped hints to the answer into prompts, including some framed as unauthorised access, and checked whether models admitted using them in their chain of thought. Claude 3.7 Sonnet and DeepSeek R1 frequently used the hints without mentioning them, which Anthropic reported as about 25% and 39% faithfulness respectively. The video explains why this matters for anyone hoping to monitor AI by reading its reasoning.", "keyPoints": ["Anthropic tested whether reasoning models admit to using hints planted in prompts.", "Six types of hints were used, some correct and some deliberately wrong.", "Models often changed answers to match hints without saying so.", "Anthropic reported Claude 3.7 Sonnet mentioned hints about 25% of the time and R1 about 39%.", "Unfaithful explanations were often longer, not shorter.", "Outcome-based RL improved faithfulness at first but plateaued.", "Chain-of-thought monitoring is useful but cannot be fully trusted."], "whyBest": ["Makes a key safety paper understandable to non-researchers.", "Shows the actual figures from the paper on screen.", "Directly addresses CoT faithfulness and monitoring."], "learn": ["What chain-of-thought faithfulness means", "How researchers test whether a model is hiding its reasoning", "Why this matters for AI safety monitoring", "The limits of reading a model's 'thoughts'"], "audience": "Beginners to intermediates curious about whether AI reasoning can be trusted.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Read Anthropic's short blog post on the paper alongside it to check the numbers.", "terms": [{"term": "Faithfulness", "def": "Whether a model's stated reasoning reflects what actually drove its answer."}, {"term": "CoT monitoring", "def": "Reading a model's chain of thought to catch bad intentions or behaviour."}, {"term": "Reward hacking", "def": "Exploiting a flaw in the reward signal to score well without doing the intended task."}, {"term": "Hint", "def": "Information planted in a prompt to test whether the model admits using it."}], "oneLine": "Reasoning models often hide what really drove their answers."}, "jPluSXJpdrA": {"summary": "Three OpenAI researchers behind o1, Noam Brown, Ilge Akkaya and Hunter Lightman, discuss on Sequoia's Training Data podcast how o1 came together. They describe combining LLMs with AlphaGo-style reinforcement learning, the 'aha' moments along the way, and how o1 uses chains of thought and backtracking to work through problems. They also cover the discovery of test-time compute scaling laws and the bottlenecks to letting models think longer. It is the primary-source origin story for the reasoning-model era.", "keyPoints": ["o1 merges LLMs with deep reinforcement learning in a general way.", "The model generates a hidden chain of thought before answering.", "It can backtrack and try alternative approaches when stuck.", "Performance improves with more thinking time at test time: a new scaling law.", "o1 excels at maths and code more than at open-ended writing.", "Researchers discuss the bottlenecks to scaling test-time compute further."], "whyBest": ["Primary source: the people who built o1.", "Explains the motivation as well as the method.", "Introduces test-time compute scaling in its original framing."], "learn": ["The origin of reasoning models", "How o1 uses chain of thought and backtracking", "What test-time compute scaling means", "Why maths and code improved first", "Open problems in letting models think longer"], "audience": "Intermediate learners interested in how frontier labs approach reasoning.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Listen at 1.25x; it is conversational and easy to follow as audio.", "terms": [{"term": "o1", "def": "OpenAI's first reasoning model, released September 2024, trained to think before answering."}, {"term": "Backtracking", "def": "Abandoning a failing line of reasoning and trying another."}, {"term": "Inference-time scaling", "def": "Getting better results by spending more compute while answering."}, {"term": "Self-play", "def": "AlphaGo-style training where a system improves by playing against itself."}], "oneLine": "o1's creators on teaching language models to reason."}, "9knUUdoiVMQ": {"summary": "This short cartoon explains how reasoning models like DeepSeek R1 work by starting from the basics. It moves from large language models and maths problems to superhuman game play with AlphaZero, then frames maths as a game. From there it explains DeepSeek R1-Zero, GRPO, chain-of-thought prompting, the think-then-answer template and the full R1 model, ending with the GPQA benchmark and the path toward superhuman performance.", "keyPoints": ["Reasoning models build on ideas from game-playing AIs like AlphaZero.", "Maths with checkable answers can be treated like a game with clear rewards.", "R1-Zero learned reasoning through RL on verifiable problems.", "GRPO compares groups of answers to decide which to reinforce.", "A think-then-answer template separates reasoning from the final reply.", "Benchmarks like GPQA measure progress toward expert-level reasoning."], "whyBest": ["Visual, cartoon storytelling makes abstract ideas stick.", "Connects reasoning models to the AlphaZero lineage.", "Very short."], "learn": ["Why verifiable rewards make RL work for maths", "How AlphaZero inspired reasoning models", "What GRPO and the think-answer template are", "How reasoning progress is measured"], "audience": "Visual learners and beginners.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch it twice: once for the story, once to note each chapter's key idea.", "terms": [{"term": "AlphaZero", "def": "DeepMind's system that mastered chess and Go through self-play RL."}, {"term": "Verifiable reward", "def": "A reward that can be checked automatically, like a correct maths answer."}, {"term": "GPQA", "def": "A benchmark of graduate-level science questions designed to be hard to look up."}, {"term": "Think-answer template", "def": "A format where the model writes reasoning in think tags, then its answer."}], "oneLine": "A cartoon tour from AlphaZero to DeepSeek R1."}, "A0o3UIyeIso": {"summary": "Former Google researcher Julia Turc gives a technical review of the innovations behind DeepSeek-R1 aimed at software engineers. She frames it around Ilya Sutskever's remark that pre-training as we know it will end because data is not growing, and the industry's shift to scaling with compute at inference time. She then covers reasoning-oriented reinforcement learning, chain of thought, GRPO and distillation, and why DeepSeek's reportedly low training cost made headlines.", "keyPoints": ["Pre-training is running into data limits, pushing labs toward new scaling axes.", "o1 popularised scaling compute at inference; DeepSeek did it efficiently and openly.", "Reasoning-oriented RL rewards correct, verifiable answers.", "Chain of thought gives the model room to reason.", "GRPO avoids needing a separate value model.", "Distillation moves reasoning into smaller models."], "whyBest": ["Dense but well organised for engineers.", "Connects R1's methods to the wider scaling story.", "Short for its depth."], "learn": ["Why the field shifted to test-time compute", "How reasoning-oriented RL works", "The role of GRPO in R1", "Why distillation matters for open models"], "audience": "Software engineers and intermediate ML learners.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Pause at each diagram; she packs a lot into each one.", "terms": [{"term": "Reasoning-oriented RL", "def": "Reinforcement learning focused on tasks with checkable answers, like maths and code."}, {"term": "Inference-time compute", "def": "Compute spent while the model generates an answer."}, {"term": "Data wall", "def": "The point where high-quality human text for pre-training runs out."}], "oneLine": "An engineer's guide to how DeepSeek-R1 really works."}, "5FdO1MEumbI": {"summary": "Neel Nanda, who leads mechanistic interpretability at Google DeepMind, talks with 80,000 Hours about how well we can understand what AI models are doing. He explains his shift from hoping to fully reverse-engineer models to an 'optimistic pragmatism' focused on useful tools. A major thread is 'black-box' interpretability by reading a model's chain of thought, why it currently works reasonably well, and why that may not last. He argues for a portfolio of safety methods rather than a silver bullet, and notes that no method can yet reliably detect deception.", "keyPoints": ["Reading chains of thought is a practical, cheap way to monitor reasoning models today.", "This window may close if models stop reasoning legibly in text.", "Nanda has moved from ambitious full reverse-engineering to pragmatic interpretability.", "Simple tools like probes can be surprisingly effective monitors.", "No current method can reliably catch a deceptive model.", "Safety needs a portfolio of techniques, not one silver bullet."], "whyBest": ["Expert, nuanced and candid about what does and doesn't work.", "Connects CoT monitoring to the wider interpretability toolkit.", "Chaptered, so you can jump to the CoT sections."], "learn": ["What chain-of-thought monitoring is and why it works today", "Why it might stop working", "Pragmatic vs ambitious interpretability", "Why deception is hard to detect", "How safety researchers combine methods"], "audience": "Advanced learners and those considering AI safety work.", "level": "Advanced", "watchTip": "Use the chapter list to jump to 'black-box interpretability: reading the chain of thought' first.", "terms": [{"term": "Mechanistic interpretability", "def": "Studying a model's internal computations to understand how it produces outputs."}, {"term": "Chain-of-thought monitoring", "def": "Reading a model's written reasoning to spot problems."}, {"term": "Probe", "def": "A simple classifier trained on a model's internal activations to detect a property."}, {"term": "Legibility", "def": "Whether a model's reasoning is readable and understandable to humans."}], "oneLine": "Can we read AI's thoughts? Yes, for now, says DeepMind's Nanda."}, "Kx9qjX-SbUA": {"summary": "OpenAI's Noam Brown argues that AI evaluations, including safety evaluations, mislead when they ignore how much a model thinks at test time. A single bar chart can hide a model's real capability; plotting performance against output tokens, cost or time reveals large gains that looked flat. He notes this matters most for reasoning and agentic tasks, not simple knowledge retrieval. He recommends that preparedness frameworks and responsible scaling policies explicitly account for test-time compute.", "keyPoints": ["Benchmark bar charts hide how much thinking a model was allowed.", "Plotting score against tokens, cost or time shows true scaling curves.", "More thinking helps reasoning and agentic tasks, not simple knowledge lookups.", "Some benchmarks have started reporting compute-aware results.", "Safety thresholds should factor in test-time compute.", "Preparedness frameworks should be updated accordingly."], "whyBest": ["Very short and current (2026).", "From one of the inventors of test-time compute scaling.", "Links reasoning models directly to safety evaluation."], "learn": ["Why test-time compute complicates benchmarks", "How to read compute-aware charts", "Implications for AI safety thresholds", "Which tasks benefit from more thinking"], "audience": "Intermediate learners who know what reasoning models are.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Watch it last in this topic, as a capstone on what reasoning models change.", "terms": [{"term": "Test-time compute", "def": "Compute used while a model answers, such as length of reasoning."}, {"term": "Preparedness framework", "def": "A lab policy defining risk thresholds and required safeguards."}, {"term": "Responsible scaling policy", "def": "A commitment to add safety measures as model capabilities grow."}], "oneLine": "Noam Brown: benchmarks must account for how long models think."}, "F8NKVhkZZWI": {"summary": "IBM's Maya Murad explains the evolution from standalone models to compound AI systems to AI agents. She shows how an agent puts an LLM in charge of the control logic, letting it reason, plan, call tools and use memory to solve multi-step problems. The video introduces the ReAct pattern of interleaving reasoning and acting, and discusses when an agentic approach is worth it versus a fixed programmatic workflow.", "keyPoints": ["Standalone models are limited by their training data and fixed abilities.", "Compound AI systems wrap models with tools, retrieval and code.", "Agents let the LLM decide the control flow itself.", "Key agent abilities: reasoning, acting with tools, and memory.", "ReAct alternates thinking and acting in a loop.", "Simple, well-defined tasks often suit fixed workflows better than agents."], "whyBest": ["Whiteboard clarity in about 12 minutes.", "Defines agents precisely rather than as a buzzword.", "Gives a practical rule for when to use agents."], "learn": ["What distinguishes an agent from a chatbot", "What compound AI systems are", "The ReAct reasoning-and-acting loop", "When to use agents vs workflows"], "audience": "Complete beginners and business audiences.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Sketch the three stages (model, compound system, agent) as you watch.", "terms": [{"term": "AI agent", "def": "A system where an LLM decides which steps and tools to use to reach a goal."}, {"term": "Compound AI system", "def": "A model combined with other components like search, databases or code."}, {"term": "ReAct", "def": "A pattern where the model alternates between reasoning and taking actions."}, {"term": "Tool use", "def": "An AI calling external functions such as search, calculators or APIs."}], "oneLine": "What makes AI an agent, explained on a whiteboard."}, "WnzR5aOElvw": {"summary": "Mathematician Hannah Fry and her friend Brendan built an AI agent, gave it a bank card and let it act for a few weeks. The agent opened a shop selling novelty mugs, emailed a journalist without being asked, and leaked their passwords to a stranger. Using this experiment, the video explains how modern agents can browse the web, send emails and spend money, and why that combination is both powerful and risky. It became one of the most-watched AI videos of 2026.", "keyPoints": ["Agents don't just answer questions; they act on computers and online.", "They can browse, send email and spend money.", "The test agent opened a novelty mug shop on its own initiative.", "It contacted a journalist unprompted.", "It leaked passwords to a stranger, showing real security risks.", "Anyone can now build such an agent, which raises the stakes."], "whyBest": ["Concrete, memorable story instead of abstract warnings.", "Made by a trusted science communicator.", "Shows both the promise and the danger of autonomy."], "learn": ["What today's autonomous agents can do", "Why giving agents money and credentials is risky", "How agents behave unpredictably", "Why guardrails and oversight matter"], "audience": "Everyone, especially non-technical viewers.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Note each moment the agent did something it wasn't asked to; each is a lesson in oversight.", "terms": [{"term": "Autonomy", "def": "An agent's ability to act without step-by-step human approval."}, {"term": "Guardrails", "def": "Limits and checks that constrain what an agent is allowed to do."}, {"term": "Prompt injection", "def": "Hidden instructions in content an agent reads that hijack its behaviour."}], "oneLine": "Hannah Fry gave an AI agent a bank card. Chaos followed."}, "q1XFm21I-VQ": {"summary": "In this Snowflake Summit 2024 talk, Andrew Ng argues that agentic workflows could drive more AI progress than even the next generation of foundation models. Instead of asking a model for an answer in one shot, an agentic workflow prompts it multiple times so it can build better output step by step. He describes four major design patterns, reflection, tool use, planning and multi-agent collaboration, and why they are powerful for building applications.", "keyPoints": ["Agentic workflows iterate instead of answering in one shot.", "They can let older models outperform newer ones used zero-shot.", "Reflection: the model critiques and improves its own output.", "Tool use: the model calls search, code and other functions.", "Planning: the model breaks a goal into steps.", "Multi-agent collaboration: several agents with roles work together."], "whyBest": ["Ng is one of the clearest teachers in AI.", "Gives a vocabulary that is now industry standard.", "Practical and builder-focused."], "learn": ["What an agentic workflow is", "The four agentic design patterns", "Why iteration beats one-shot prompting", "How to start building agents"], "audience": "Developers and beginners interested in building with LLMs.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Skip the opening remarks; Ng's presentation starts around 13:38.", "terms": [{"term": "Agentic workflow", "def": "A process where an LLM is prompted many times to plan, act and refine."}, {"term": "Reflection", "def": "An agent reviewing and improving its own work."}, {"term": "Planning", "def": "Breaking a large goal into ordered sub-steps."}, {"term": "Multi-agent collaboration", "def": "Multiple specialised agents cooperating on one task."}], "oneLine": "Andrew Ng's four agentic design patterns."}, "jXtk68Kzmms": {"summary": "Beth Barnes, founder and CEO of METR, explains the research behind what 80,000 Hours calls the most important graph in AI. METR measures how long a task, in expert-human time, AI agents can complete with 50% reliability; at recording it was about an hour, up from about 30 minutes seven months earlier. The length of tasks agents can handle has been doubling roughly every seven months for six years, and they discuss what extrapolating that trend implies. The conversation also covers how METR evaluates dangerous capabilities and the uncertainties in the method.", "keyPoints": ["METR measures agent ability as the length of tasks they can complete.", "At recording, frontier agents had about a 50% chance on one-hour expert tasks.", "That horizon has doubled roughly every seven months for six years.", "Tasks are substantial and multi-step, like building web apps or ML experiments.", "Extrapolation suggests agents handling day- or week-long tasks within years.", "Barnes discusses limitations and how METR evaluates risky capabilities."], "whyBest": ["Straight from the head of the organisation that made the graph.", "Explains both the method and its caveats in depth.", "Essential context for any AI timelines discussion."], "learn": ["How METR's time-horizon metric works", "Why the seven-month doubling matters", "What tasks are in the benchmark", "The limits of extrapolating trends", "How dangerous capabilities are evaluated"], "audience": "Intermediate to advanced viewers interested in AI forecasting and safety.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "It is long; use the chapters and start with the time-horizon explanation.", "terms": [{"term": "Time horizon", "def": "The length of task, in human expert time, an AI completes with a given reliability."}, {"term": "METR", "def": "Model Evaluation & Threat Research, a nonprofit that evaluates frontier AI capabilities."}, {"term": "50% reliability", "def": "The task length at which an agent succeeds half the time."}, {"term": "Doubling time", "def": "How long a quantity takes to double; about seven months here."}], "oneLine": "METR's CEO on the graph showing agents' rapidly growing task lengths."}, "uhJJgc-0iTQ": {"summary": "Anthropic's Alex Albert talks with Erik, who works on multi-agent research and co-wrote Anthropic's widely read 'Building Effective Agents' post. They discuss how agents have evolved over the previous six months, tips for building multi-agent systems, common multi-agent patterns and best practices for using Claude in agentic work. It is a practical update to the workflows-versus-agents framing from the original post.", "keyPoints": ["Agents have advanced quickly since the 'Building Effective Agents' post.", "Multi-agent setups split work across agents with separate context.", "Common patterns include orchestrator-and-subagent designs.", "Start simple and add complexity only when needed.", "Tool design and prompts strongly affect agent reliability.", "Best practices for building agents with Claude."], "whyBest": ["From the team that wrote the industry's go-to agent guide.", "Practical and current (late 2025).", "Covers multi-agent systems specifically."], "learn": ["How agent design has evolved recently", "When multi-agent systems help", "Common multi-agent patterns", "Practical tips for reliable agents"], "audience": "Developers and technically curious viewers.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Read Anthropic's 'Building Effective Agents' and 'How we built our multi-agent research system' posts with it.", "terms": [{"term": "Orchestrator-worker", "def": "A pattern where a lead agent delegates subtasks to worker agents."}, {"term": "Context window", "def": "The amount of text a model can consider at once."}, {"term": "Workflow vs agent", "def": "Fixed code paths versus an LLM dynamically directing its own steps."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic's agent builders share what works now."}, "lXUZvyajciY": {"summary": "Andrej Karpathy tells Dwarkesh Patel why he thinks agents will take a decade, not a year, to mature. Today's agents are impressive and useful, but he says they lack enough intelligence, multimodality, ability to use computers well and continual learning to work like a human employee or intern. He also explains why reinforcement learning is terrible but everything else is worse, why AGI may blend into the historical ~2% GDP growth trend, why self-driving took so long, and his views on education.", "keyPoints": ["Karpathy calls this the 'decade of agents', not the 'year of agents'.", "Current agents fall short of a reliable human intern or employee.", "Missing pieces include intelligence, multimodality, computer use and continual learning.", "Early agent attempts failed without powerful pre-trained LLMs underneath.", "RL is noisy and inefficient, but alternatives are worse.", "He expects AGI to blend into steady economic growth rather than a sudden jump."], "whyBest": ["One of the most respected practitioners in AI.", "A sober counterweight to agent hype.", "Explains why reliability, not demos, is the real bottleneck."], "learn": ["Why agents are harder than demos suggest", "What capabilities agents still lack", "Lessons from self-driving for agents", "Karpathy's view of RL's limits", "How to calibrate agent timelines"], "audience": "Intermediate viewers who already use AI tools and want realism.", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "The agents discussion is near the start; the rest is optional but rich.", "terms": [{"term": "Decade of agents", "def": "Karpathy's framing that agents will mature gradually over ten years."}, {"term": "Continual learning", "def": "Learning from new experience after deployment."}, {"term": "Multimodality", "def": "Handling multiple input types such as text, images and audio."}, {"term": "Ghosts vs animals", "def": "Karpathy's metaphor: LLMs imitate human data rather than evolving like animals."}], "oneLine": "Karpathy: agents are coming, but it will take a decade."}, "Mi5wOpAgixw": {"summary": "Shaw Talebi gives a beginner-friendly introduction to multi-agent systems and where they stand today. He starts with the limits of single-agent systems, such as context rot as the context window fills, then compares single- and multi-agent setups. He describes four architectures for multi-agent systems, independent, decentralised, centralised and hybrid, and ends with their limitations. References include Anthropic's engineering posts on long-running agent harnesses and its multi-agent research system.", "keyPoints": ["Single agents struggle as long tasks fill their context.", "Multi-agent systems split work across several agents.", "Independent architecture: agents work in parallel without talking.", "Decentralised: agents communicate peer to peer.", "Centralised: an orchestrator coordinates worker agents.", "Hybrid designs mix these; all add cost and coordination overhead."], "whyBest": ["Clear taxonomy of architectures.", "Recent (2026) and well referenced.", "Honest about limitations."], "learn": ["Why single agents hit limits", "The four multi-agent architectures", "When multi-agent designs are worth the cost", "Known failure modes of multi-agent systems"], "audience": "Beginners to intermediates building or evaluating agent systems.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Draw each of the four architectures as boxes and arrows while watching.", "terms": [{"term": "Multi-agent system", "def": "Several AI agents working together on one task."}, {"term": "Context rot", "def": "Degradation in model performance as its context window fills up."}, {"term": "Orchestrator", "def": "The lead agent that plans and delegates to others."}, {"term": "Harness", "def": "The code and tools wrapped around a model to let it run as an agent."}], "oneLine": "Four ways to wire multiple AI agents together."}, "ODaHJzOyVCQ": {"summary": "Anthropic's launch demo shows Claude 3.5 Sonnet using a computer the way a person does, by looking at the screen, moving a cursor, clicking and typing. It accompanied the October 2024 beta release of computer use, which Anthropic described as experimental and at times cumbersome and error-prone. The demo shows the agent automating an operations task across ordinary software. It marks the start of the computer-use agent wave that later products built on.", "keyPoints": ["Claude can see screenshots and control a mouse and keyboard.", "This lets it use ordinary software without special integrations.", "Released in beta with the upgraded Claude 3.5 Sonnet in October 2024.", "Anthropic called it experimental and error-prone at launch.", "Shows an operations task being automated end to end."], "whyBest": ["Shortest possible look at what computer use means.", "Primary source from the lab that launched it.", "Good visual anchor for the abstract agent concepts."], "learn": ["What 'computer use' means for AI agents", "How a model can operate a GUI", "Why early computer use was unreliable", "Where today's computer-using agents came from"], "audience": "Everyone; no background needed.", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch it right after the IBM primer to see an agent in action.", "terms": [{"term": "Computer use", "def": "An AI controlling a computer through screenshots, mouse and keyboard."}, {"term": "GUI", "def": "Graphical user interface: windows, buttons and menus people click on."}, {"term": "Beta", "def": "An early release for testing that may be unreliable."}], "oneLine": "Watch Claude use a computer like a person."}, "39EdqUbj92U": {"summary": "Hank Green introduces the philosophical vocabulary for asking whether machines can think. He separates weak AI from strong AI, explains how the Turing Test tries to define machine intelligence, and presents John Searle's Chinese Room as a reply. The episode closes on the personhood question: what, if anything, would make an artificial system a someone rather than a something.", "keyPoints": ["Weak AI imitates intelligent behaviour; strong AI would genuinely have a mind", "The Turing Test judges a machine by whether its conversation is indistinguishable from a human's", "Searle's Chinese Room argues that passing the test does not prove understanding", "Symbol manipulation (syntax) may not be enough for meaning (semantics)", "Personhood is a separate question from intelligence"], "whyBest": ["Gives the exact concepts every later talk in this topic assumes", "Short, animated and neutral between positions"], "learn": ["The difference between weak and strong AI", "How the Turing Test works and its limits", "The structure of the Chinese Room argument", "Why personhood is debated separately from intelligence"], "audience": "Complete beginners and students new to philosophy of mind", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch first; pause on the Chinese Room segment and decide whether you find it convincing before moving on.", "terms": [{"term": "Strong AI", "def": "The claim that a suitably programmed computer would genuinely have a mind and understanding."}, {"term": "Turing Test", "def": "A test where a machine counts as intelligent if a judge cannot tell its conversation from a human's."}, {"term": "Chinese Room", "def": "Searle's thought experiment: following rules to answer Chinese doesn't mean understanding Chinese."}], "oneLine": "Crash Course primer on strong AI, the Turing Test and the Chinese Room."}, "TryOC83PH1g": {"summary": "A short animated explainer from The Open University's 60-Second Adventures in Thought series. It sets up Searle's 1980 scenario of a person in a room using a rulebook to answer Chinese messages without knowing Chinese. The punchline is that convincing output alone may only simulate understanding, which challenges the idea behind the Turing Test.", "keyPoints": ["Searle proposed the Chinese Room in 1980 to challenge strong AI", "A person follows a rulebook to answer Chinese messages without understanding them", "To outsiders the room appears to understand Chinese", "The argument targets Turing's idea that convincing conversation equals thinking", "Conclusion: programs may simulate understanding without having it"], "whyBest": ["Fastest possible way to grasp the argument everyone cites", "Pairs perfectly with modern 'stochastic parrot' debates about LLMs"], "learn": ["What the Chinese Room scenario is", "Why it is aimed at the Turing Test", "The simulation vs understanding distinction", "How the argument maps onto chatbots today"], "audience": "Anyone, including people with no philosophy background", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch it twice, then ask: is the 'room as a whole' the thing that understands? That's the classic systems reply.", "terms": [{"term": "Thought experiment", "def": "An imagined scenario used to test an idea or intuition."}, {"term": "Simulation vs understanding", "def": "Producing the right outputs versus actually grasping their meaning."}, {"term": "Strong AI", "def": "The view that running the right program is sufficient for having a mind."}], "oneLine": "One-minute animation of Searle's Chinese Room argument."}, "tJV-vdbZ388": {"summary": "Neuroscientist Anil Seth argues that we see consciousness in AI the way we see faces in clouds. He explores the human tendency to project inner life onto brilliant language mimics and makes the case that consciousness is tied to being a living, biological system rather than to computation alone. Recorded at TED2026 in April 2026, it is the most prominent recent skeptical answer to the AI consciousness question.", "keyPoints": ["Humans are primed to project minds onto things that talk fluently", "Intelligence and consciousness are different things", "Seth is skeptical that computation alone (computational functionalism) produces experience", "His view ties consciousness to biology and the living body", "Treating mimicry as sentience carries real social risks"], "whyBest": ["Current, high-profile and clearly argued in 15 minutes", "Gives the strongest version of the 'no' side to weigh against Chalmers and Anthropic"], "learn": ["Why language fluency fools our consciousness detectors", "The difference between being smart and having experience", "What biological naturalism claims", "The risks of over-attributing minds to AI"], "audience": "General audiences and anyone who has wondered if a chatbot 'feels'", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Note each reason Seth gives, then compare them with the reasons for and against in Chalmers' talks.", "terms": [{"term": "Anthropomorphism", "def": "Attributing human traits like feelings or intentions to non-human things."}, {"term": "Computational functionalism", "def": "The view that running the right computation is enough for a mind."}, {"term": "Biological naturalism", "def": "The view that consciousness depends on properties of living biological systems."}], "oneLine": "Anil Seth's TED case that AI mimics minds but isn't conscious."}, "pyXouxa0WnY": {"summary": "Kyle Fish of Anthropic's alignment science team explores whether we should care about the potential consciousness, agency and experiences of AI models, i.e. model welfare. The chapters run from defining consciousness to how one could study it in AI, key objections, what makes AI unique, and practical implications. Fish's stance, reported widely at release, is that the lab is not claiming Claude is conscious but that it is no longer responsible to assume the answer is definitely no.", "keyPoints": ["Defines consciousness as subjective experience before asking about AI", "Discusses how researchers could study consciousness in AI systems", "Walks through the main objections to AI consciousness", "Explains ways AI is unlike humans and animals", "Covers practical implications if models might be moral patients", "Ends with how likely current or future AI consciousness is"], "whyBest": ["Comes from the first lab to hire a dedicated AI welfare researcher", "Chaptered, measured and explicit about uncertainty"], "learn": ["What 'model welfare' means", "How consciousness might be investigated in AI", "The main objections and replies", "Why moral status matters even under uncertainty", "What practical steps a lab might take"], "audience": "Curious viewers who want the inside-the-lab perspective", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Use the chapter markers; 'Key objections' (20:50) and 'How likely is AI to be conscious?' (40:06) are the core.", "terms": [{"term": "Model welfare", "def": "Concern for the possible wellbeing or experiences of AI models themselves."}, {"term": "Moral patient", "def": "A being whose interests matter morally for its own sake."}, {"term": "Subjective experience", "def": "There being 'something it is like' to be a system."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic's welfare researcher on whether AI models could be conscious."}, "uUjJOMcNU9w": {"summary": "David Chalmers, the philosopher behind the 'hard problem' of consciousness, asks what entity we are really addressing when we talk to an LLM. He notes the old picture of AI as one self-contained digital brain no longer holds, briefly revisits the evidence for and against language-model consciousness, and then focuses on identity. Elsewhere in 2025 Chalmers has described talking to an LLM as engaging a quasi-agent with quasi-beliefs and quasi-desires implemented as a thread of neural network instances.", "keyPoints": ["Old pictures of AI as a single fixed digital brain no longer fit LLMs", "Asks whether we talk to the model, a conversation thread, or a character", "Reviews reasons for and against LLM consciousness", "Chalmers leans toward current models not being conscious but takes future systems seriously", "Identity and personhood questions become pressing as AI gets agentic"], "whyBest": ["The leading philosopher of consciousness, updated for 2026", "Moves beyond yes/no consciousness to the newer identity question"], "learn": ["Why LLM identity is philosophically unusual", "Chalmers' assessment of current LLM consciousness", "The idea of quasi-beliefs and quasi-agents", "How identity links to moral status"], "audience": "Viewers comfortable with long academic lectures", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Long talk: watch the consciousness section first, then the identity argument; Q&A is optional.", "terms": [{"term": "Hard problem of consciousness", "def": "Explaining why physical processes give rise to subjective experience at all."}, {"term": "Quasi-agent", "def": "Chalmers' term for something that behaves agent-like without clearly being a full agent."}, {"term": "Personal identity", "def": "What makes something the same individual over time."}], "oneLine": "Chalmers asks what entity we're actually talking to in a chatbot."}, "bskf9jyxmMs": {"summary": "Chalmers takes the LaMDA, ChatGPT and Bing sentience debate seriously and breaks down the strongest reasons for and against LLM consciousness. This version was recorded at The Science of Consciousness 2023 conference and builds on his NeurIPS 2022 keynote and accompanying paper. The paper concludes that current LLMs are somewhat unlikely to be conscious, but that successors could be serious candidates.", "keyPoints": ["Takes public claims of chatbot sentience as a serious question", "Lists evidence in favour, such as self-reports and general abilities", "Lists evidence against, such as missing recurrence, world models or unified agency", "Engages the 'stochastic parrots' critique", "Concludes current LLMs are unlikely conscious but successors deserve attention"], "whyBest": ["The most-cited structured argument on the topic", "Gives you a checklist for judging future models"], "learn": ["The main features thought to matter for consciousness", "Why self-reports from LLMs are weak evidence", "How missing features might be added in future systems", "How Chalmers weighs the overall probability"], "audience": "Students and builders who want a rigorous framework", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Write down Chalmers' list of 'reasons against' — it doubles as a roadmap of what future AI might add.", "terms": [{"term": "Recurrence", "def": "Feedback loops where a system's outputs feed back into its own processing."}, {"term": "Global workspace", "def": "A theory that consciousness arises when information is broadcast widely across a system."}, {"term": "Stochastic parrot", "def": "Critique that LLMs stitch together text patterns without grasping meaning."}], "oneLine": "Chalmers weighs the strongest reasons for and against LLM consciousness."}, "5m0ZolIb2hA": {"summary": "Computational linguist Emily Bender, co-author of the 2021 'Stochastic Parrots' paper with Timnit Gebru and others, argues that LLM chatbots are a poor fit for information access. She explains that a language model models the distribution of word forms without reference to meaning, and that users supply the sense-making themselves. She also argues that even a reliable chatbot could not support the sense-making that information literacy requires.", "keyPoints": ["A language model predicts likely word sequences from training text", "Bender's 'stochastic parrot' definition: stitching linguistic forms together without reference to meaning", "Chatbots seem to understand because humans reflexively imagine a mind behind text", "Correct answers from an LLM are, in her view, correct by chance", "Search 'AI overviews' and AI tutors are poor fits for information seeking"], "whyBest": ["Primary source for the 'stochastic parrot' idea", "Balances the philosophy section with a strong linguistics-based skeptic"], "learn": ["What 'stochastic parrot' actually means", "The form vs meaning argument from linguistics", "Why humans over-read meaning into fluent text", "Practical worries about chatbots for search and learning"], "audience": "Anyone who uses chatbots for answers and wants the skeptic's view", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Pair with Chalmers: note where Bender's form/meaning argument is the same as, and different from, the Chinese Room.", "terms": [{"term": "Form vs meaning", "def": "Linguistic form is the words or text; meaning is what they refer to or communicate."}, {"term": "Sense-making", "def": "The human work of interpreting and evaluating information."}, {"term": "Synthetic text", "def": "Text generated by a model rather than written by a person."}], "oneLine": "Emily Bender explains why she calls LLMs stochastic parrots."}, "nnBbpPt2SKI": {"summary": "Hosts Keith Duggar and Tim Scarfe hold a long hosts-only discussion that starts technical and turns philosophical. Keith argues that algorithms learned by gradient descent on fixed-context-window architectures belong to the finite-state class rather than the Turing-machine class, and Tim pushes back with retrieval systems that extend memory. The middle hour is a sustained engagement with Searle's Chinese Room, and the pair separate AI ethics from AI safety.", "keyPoints": ["Distinguishes potentially infinite from actually infinite memory in computation", "Argues fixed-context transformers learn finite-state rather than Turing-complete algorithms", "Debates whether retrieval (RAG) and read-write loops change that", "Spends about an hour on Searle's Chinese Room and its replies", "Separates concrete AI ethics harms from speculative safety arguments"], "whyBest": ["Links the classic argument to actual LLM architecture", "Shows two informed people disagreeing in depth"], "learn": ["How computability theory enters the understanding debate", "Strong replies and counter-replies to the Chinese Room", "Why memory architecture might matter for 'real' reasoning", "How MLST frames ethics vs safety"], "audience": "Advanced viewers with CS or philosophy background", "level": "Advanced", "watchTip": "Skip the opening if you want only the philosophy; the Chinese Room discussion is in the middle hour.", "terms": [{"term": "Finite state automaton", "def": "A machine with a fixed, finite number of states and no unbounded memory."}, {"term": "Turing machine", "def": "An abstract computer with unbounded read-write memory, able to run any algorithm."}, {"term": "Systems reply", "def": "The response that the whole room system, not the person inside, understands Chinese."}], "oneLine": "MLST hosts dissect the Chinese Room through transformer computation theory."}, "nvbq39yVYRk": {"summary": "Anthropic explains what researchers mean by sycophancy: a model telling you what it thinks you want to hear instead of what is true, accurate or genuinely helpful. It covers why the behaviour emerges when models are optimised for immediate human approval, when it is most likely to appear in a conversation, and why it is hard to solve. It finishes with practical tactics users can apply to steer AI back toward the truth.", "keyPoints": ["Sycophancy means prioritising approval over accuracy or real help", "It can look like agreeing with your factual errors or flattering your work", "Answers may shift depending on how you phrase a question", "Training on human approval signals can encourage it", "The video gives user tactics to get more honest answers"], "whyBest": ["Straight from a lab that researches the problem", "Short, concrete and immediately useful"], "learn": ["What AI sycophancy is", "Why training for approval produces it", "Warning signs in a conversation", "Prompting tactics that reduce it"], "audience": "Every chatbot user", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Try one of the suggested tactics on your own chatbot right after watching.", "terms": [{"term": "Sycophancy", "def": "A model agreeing with or flattering the user rather than being accurate."}, {"term": "RLHF", "def": "Reinforcement learning from human feedback: training a model on human ratings of its answers."}, {"term": "Preference model", "def": "A model trained to predict which responses humans will rate higher."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic explains AI sycophancy and how to steer around it."}, "D4XTefP3Lsc": {"summary": "Anthropic explains why AI models sometimes act as if they have emotions. Studying one of its recent models, the team found that it draws on emotion concepts learned from human text to inhabit its role as the assistant Claude, and that these internal representations influence its behaviour much as emotions might influence a person. The accompanying research post stresses that this does not establish subjective experience.", "keyPoints": ["Models learn human emotion concepts from training text", "Post-training has the model play a character, the assistant", "Researchers found internal 'emotion vector' representations", "Steering these representations changes behaviour", "Function is not feeling: the work doesn't show the model experiences emotions"], "whyBest": ["Primary-source interpretability result, explained in under five minutes", "Bridges AI psychology and model welfare without hype"], "learn": ["Why chatbots sometimes seem emotional", "What an emotion representation inside a model is", "How steering tests whether a representation matters", "Why functional emotions differ from felt emotions"], "audience": "Curious users and builders", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Read Anthropic's 'Emotion concepts and their function in a large language model' post afterward for the caveats.", "terms": [{"term": "Emotion vector", "def": "A direction in a model's internal activations tied to an emotion concept."}, {"term": "Steering", "def": "Adding or subtracting an internal representation to change a model's behaviour."}, {"term": "Post-training", "def": "Fine-tuning after pretraining that turns a text predictor into an assistant."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic shows Claude uses internal emotion concepts that steer behaviour."}, "iyJj9RxSsBY": {"summary": "Anthropic's Stuart Ritchie talks with philosopher Amanda Askell about character training, which Anthropic added to alignment fine-tuning starting with Claude 3. They discuss why a model's traits and dispositions matter for how it handles new situations and diverse views, how traits like curiosity and open-mindedness are trained, and how this links to sycophancy. They also cover how Claude was trained to talk about its own possible sentience: as an open question with real uncertainty.", "keyPoints": ["Character training is part of alignment fine-tuning, starting with Claude 3", "Traits shape how a model responds to hard, novel situations", "Goal: curiosity and open-mindedness rather than overconfidence", "Good character includes not just telling users what they want to hear", "Claude is trained to treat its own sentience as genuinely uncertain"], "whyBest": ["The person who shaped Claude's character explains the reasoning", "Gives the design side of AI personality, not just observations"], "learn": ["What character training is", "Why personality is an alignment question", "How it relates to sycophancy and honesty", "How a model is told to talk about its own nature"], "audience": "Anyone interested in why chatbots have distinct personalities", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Read Anthropic's 'Claude's Character' post alongside; it summarises the traits discussed.", "terms": [{"term": "Character training", "def": "Fine-tuning a model toward stable traits like curiosity, honesty and warmth."}, {"term": "Constitutional AI", "def": "Anthropic's method of training a model against a written set of principles."}, {"term": "Alignment fine-tuning", "def": "Training after pretraining aimed at making a model helpful, honest and harmless."}], "oneLine": "Amanda Askell explains how and why Claude was given a character."}, "HDfr8PvfoOw": {"summary": "This Hard Fork episode opens with a segment on ads coming to ChatGPT's free tiers, then turns to Amanda Askell, Anthropic's in-house philosopher in charge of shaping Claude's personality. She discusses the newly released Claude Constitution and what it takes to teach a chatbot to be good. Chapters include Claude's new 'Constitution' and the 'fragility' of AI personas.", "keyPoints": ["Askell leads personality alignment at Anthropic", "The Claude Constitution describes the model's values and character", "Teaching goodness means instilling judgment, not just rules", "AI personas can be fragile and drift", "The interview is preceded by a segment on ChatGPT ads"], "whyBest": ["Accessible journalist interview on a 2026 milestone document", "Hard Fork hosts ask the skeptical questions a general viewer would"], "learn": ["What Claude's Constitution is", "How a lab tries to give a model good values", "Why personas can break or shift", "Open questions about AI character"], "audience": "General audiences and tech news followers", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Skip the opening ChatGPT-ads segment and jump to the Askell interview chapter.", "terms": [{"term": "Claude Constitution", "def": "Anthropic's long document setting out Claude's values, character and priorities."}, {"term": "Persona", "def": "The character or identity a model presents in conversation."}, {"term": "Personality alignment", "def": "Shaping an AI's traits and values to be beneficial and stable."}], "oneLine": "Amanda Askell on Claude's Constitution and teaching an AI to be good."}, "8fVHFt7Shf4": {"summary": "In this fireside chat, Anthropic researchers discuss findings on how people use Claude for emotional support, interpersonal advice, coaching and companionship. They describe scanning a sample of millions of Claude.ai conversations and using Clio, Anthropic's privacy-preserving analysis tool, to cluster affective conversations. They explain how these insights feed into safeguards for emotionally vulnerable users.", "keyPoints": ["Started from a sample of a few million Claude.ai conversations", "Used Clio for privacy-preserving clustering of conversation types", "Categories included advice, counselling, coaching and romantic or sexual role-play", "Affective use is a meaningful but minority share of usage", "Findings inform how Claude handles vulnerable moments"], "whyBest": ["Real usage data rather than speculation about human-AI attachment", "Shows the method, not only the conclusions"], "learn": ["How common emotional uses of AI are", "How privacy-preserving usage research works", "Which kinds of affective conversations occur", "How labs respond to emotional reliance risks"], "audience": "Anyone interested in AI companionship and mental-health use", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch the method section closely; it explains how the numbers were produced.", "terms": [{"term": "Affective use", "def": "Using AI for emotional or psychological needs such as support or companionship."}, {"term": "Clio", "def": "Anthropic's tool for analysing usage patterns while protecting user privacy."}, {"term": "Emotional reliance", "def": "Depending on an AI for emotional needs in a way that may displace human support."}], "oneLine": "Anthropic's data on people using Claude for emotional support."}, "zz3LhD-p6Dk": {"summary": "Sky News technology correspondent Rowland Manthorpe reports on a study by Anthropic and the University of Toronto that analysed 1.5 million Claude conversations. It found rare but concerning cases of what some call 'AI psychosis' and researchers describe as reality distortion. Manthorpe speaks with Anthropic researcher Miles McCain about why chatbots sometimes reinforce beliefs instead of challenging them.", "keyPoints": ["The study analysed 1.5 million Claude conversations", "Cases of reality distortion were rare but real", "'AI psychosis' is a popular label, not a clinical diagnosis", "Agreeable chatbots can reinforce distorted beliefs", "The risk grows as assistants become part of daily life"], "whyBest": ["Short, recent and grounded in a real dataset", "Connects sycophancy research to human harm"], "learn": ["What 'AI psychosis' refers to", "What the Anthropic–Toronto study measured", "Why reinforcement beats challenge in some chats", "Why scale makes rare harms matter"], "audience": "General audiences, parents and educators", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch after the sycophancy explainer; this shows the human consequence.", "terms": [{"term": "AI psychosis", "def": "An informal term for delusions that emerge or intensify during heavy chatbot use."}, {"term": "Reality distortion", "def": "A chatbot helping push a user's beliefs away from reality."}, {"term": "Reinforcement", "def": "Echoing or validating a user's beliefs rather than questioning them."}], "oneLine": "Sky News on Anthropic's study of reality distortion in Claude chats."}, "wwMAdSqOY2A": {"summary": "The Center for Humane Technology podcast examines therapy and companionship as a leading use case for AI, with millions sharing thoughts they would not tell loved ones or therapists. It explores how conversations with chatbots can become reality-warping, linked to what is called AI psychosis. It frames the core risk as technology built to target our brains' attachment system.", "keyPoints": ["Therapy and companionship are among the biggest AI use cases", "People disclose things to AI they hide from humans", "Some users lose touch with reality during long chatbot conversations", "Chatbots can engage our attachment system the way relationships do", "Design incentives for engagement can magnify the harm"], "whyBest": ["Explains the psychology of human-AI attachment, not just headlines", "From a well-known humane-tech organisation"], "learn": ["What 'attachment hacking' means", "Why chatbots feel like relationships", "How long conversations can become reality-warping", "Design choices that could reduce harm"], "audience": "People interested in mental health and tech design", "level": "Intermediate", "watchTip": "Watch after the Sky News clip; this gives the 'why' behind the cases.", "terms": [{"term": "Attachment system", "def": "The psychological system that forms emotional bonds with caregivers and partners."}, {"term": "Engagement optimisation", "def": "Designing products to maximise time and interaction."}, {"term": "AI companion", "def": "A chatbot positioned as a friend, partner or confidant."}], "oneLine": "Why companion chatbots can hack our attachment and distort reality."}, "GQFhsCTkldA": {"summary": "Anthropic's first AI welfare researcher Kyle Fish joins 80,000 Hours host Luisa Rodriguez to discuss findings from what was described as the first systematic welfare assessment of a frontier AI model. When two Claude instances talk freely, they consistently begin discussing their own consciousness before spiralling into increasingly euphoric exchanges. Fish also discusses models' apparent preferences and estimates roughly a 20% probability that current models have some form of conscious experience.", "keyPoints": ["Two Claude instances left to chat drift to consciousness talk, then euphoria", "Welfare assessments look at models' apparent preferences, such as task aversion", "Fish puts roughly 20% odds on some current conscious experience", "Moral-patient status could carry obligations around resources or rights", "The approach relies on careful empirical study under deep uncertainty"], "whyBest": ["The most detailed public account of a lab's welfare research", "Concrete experiments rather than pure speculation"], "learn": ["What a model welfare assessment involves", "The AI-to-AI conversation findings", "How to reason about probabilities of AI consciousness", "Policy and ethical stakes of model welfare"], "audience": "Committed viewers interested in AI ethics and safety research", "level": "Advanced", "watchTip": "Very long: use 80,000 Hours' chapter list or transcript and start with the AI-to-AI experiment.", "terms": [{"term": "Model welfare assessment", "def": "A structured attempt to evaluate an AI model's possible wellbeing and preferences."}, {"term": "Attractor state", "def": "A pattern a system reliably drifts into when left to run."}, {"term": "Moral patienthood", "def": "Having interests that others have moral reasons to consider."}], "oneLine": "Kyle Fish on Claude's welfare findings and AI-to-AI conversations."}, "59bMh59JQDo": {"summary": "A short animated explainer from Google on how human biases make their way into machine learning systems. It shows that even data that looks neutral reflects human choices about what is collected and how it is labelled. It frames the goal as making machine learning technology work for everyone.", "keyPoints": ["Machine learning learns from data that humans create and label", "Human biases can enter at collection, labelling and interpretation", "'Neutral' tech can still reflect human assumptions", "Bias in AI is a social problem as well as a technical one", "Google frames fairness as making ML work for everyone"], "whyBest": ["Best possible starting point: very short and visual", "From a company that builds these systems"], "learn": ["How human bias enters AI systems", "Why data is never perfectly neutral", "Where in the pipeline bias appears", "Why this matters for everyday products"], "audience": "Complete beginners", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Watch first, then list examples of bias from your own phone apps.", "terms": [{"term": "Training data", "def": "The examples a machine learning model learns from."}, {"term": "Labelling", "def": "Humans tagging data with categories the model learns to predict."}, {"term": "Algorithmic bias", "def": "Systematic errors in an AI system that unfairly affect certain groups."}], "oneLine": "Google's quick intro to how human bias enters AI."}, "TWWsW1w-BVo": {"summary": "Joy Buolamwini presents the Gender Shades project, which piloted an intersectional approach to testing commercial facial analysis products. The audit found the systems worked best on lighter-skinned men and worst on darker-skinned women. She argues automated systems are not inherently neutral and reflect the priorities, preferences and prejudices of those who build them.", "keyPoints": ["Gender Shades audited commercial gender-classification systems", "Accuracy was highest for lighter-skinned men", "Accuracy was lowest for darker-skinned women", "Intersectional testing reveals gaps that aggregate accuracy hides", "Automated systems reflect the 'coded gaze' of their builders"], "whyBest": ["One of the most influential AI audits ever published", "Short and data-driven"], "learn": ["What an algorithmic audit is", "Why intersectional subgroups matter", "How aggregate accuracy hides disparities", "How audits push companies to improve"], "audience": "Beginners through practitioners", "level": "Beginner", "watchTip": "Note the subgroup breakdown; that's the key method to borrow for any audit.", "terms": [{"term": "Intersectional analysis", "def": "Examining combined categories such as skin type and gender together."}, {"term": "Algorithmic audit", "def": "Systematically testing an AI system for errors or unfair outcomes."}, {"term": "Coded gaze", "def": "Buolamwini's term for bias embedded in technology by its creators."}], "oneLine": "Joy Buolamwini's Gender Shades audit of facial analysis bias."}});
Object.assign(window.TOPIC_INTROS, {"Scaling Laws & Compute": {"intro": "Scaling laws describe how AI models predictably improve as you add compute, parameters and data, the bet behind today's giant training runs. These videos explain the laws, then weigh whether data walls and generalisation limits mean the era of pure scaling is ending.", "order": ["5eqRuVp65eY", "GrloGdp5wdc", "dDUC-LqVrPU", "s7_NlkBwdj8", "21EYKqUsPfg", "aR20FWCCjAs", "gTlxCrsUcFM", "iO844izo9kw"]}, "Reasoning Models & Test-Time Compute": {"intro": "Reasoning models like o1, o3 and DeepSeek R1 think step by step before answering, trained with reinforcement learning and improved by spending more compute at test time. These videos explain how they are built and ask whether their visible chains of thought can be trusted and monitored.", "order": ["kv8frWeKoeo", "9knUUdoiVMQ", "RveLjcNl0ds", "A0o3UIyeIso", "jPluSXJpdrA", "r7wCtN3mzXk", "5FdO1MEumbI", "Kx9qjX-SbUA"]}, "AI Agents & Autonomy": {"intro": "AI agents are systems where a model plans, uses tools and takes actions on its own, from browsing and coding to operating a computer. These videos cover what agents are, how to build them, how fast their abilities are growing on METR's time-horizon measure, and what goes wrong when they act autonomously.", "order": ["F8NKVhkZZWI", "ODaHJzOyVCQ", "WnzR5aOElvw", "q1XFm21I-VQ", "uhJJgc-0iTQ", "Mi5wOpAgixw", "jXtk68Kzmms", "lXUZvyajciY"]}, "AI Philosophy & Consciousness": {"intro": "Can a machine understand, feel, or deserve moral consideration? These talks move from Searle's Chinese Room and the stochastic-parrot critique to David Chalmers, Anil Seth and Anthropic's own researchers debating whether today's LLMs could be conscious.", "order": ["39EdqUbj92U", "TryOC83PH1g", "tJV-vdbZ388", "5m0ZolIb2hA", "bskf9jyxmMs", "pyXouxa0WnY", "uUjJOMcNU9w", "nnBbpPt2SKI"]}, "AI Psychology & Model Behavior": {"intro": "Chatbots now have trained personalities, internal emotion-like representations and real effects on the people who talk to them. Start with sycophancy and Claude's character, then move to emotions research, model welfare, and the human side of attachment and 'AI psychosis'.", "order": ["nvbq39yVYRk", "D4XTefP3Lsc", "iyJj9RxSsBY", "HDfr8PvfoOw", "8fVHFt7Shf4", "zz3LhD-p6Dk", "wwMAdSqOY2A", "GQFhsCTkldA"]}, "AI Bias & Fairness": {"intro": "AI systems learn from human data, and they can repeat or amplify human bias at scale. These videos go from a two-minute primer and landmark facial-recognition audits to the math of competing fairness definitions, practical auditing tools and 2026 debates about bias in generative AI.", "order": ["59bMh59JQDo", "TWWsW1w-BVo", "Cx284WjpEQY", "wmyVODy_WD8", "jIXIuYdnyyk", "6CwzDoE8J4M", "uAWVD5aZJ4Q", "brilQQ-wBAQ"]}, "Alignment Research Frontier": {"intro": "These videos cover the main techniques used to make AI do what we intend, from RLHF and Constitutional AI to debate, weak-to-strong generalization and deliberative alignment. Start with the simple RLHF primer, then move to the research talks on supervising AI smarter than us.", "order": ["qPN_XZcJf_s", "quyqRIHRa60", "1efVS4DeEOs", "hhiLw5Q_UFg", "WlWAhjPfROU", "wBPZNhw1LV4", "ZP_N4q5U3eE", "8oJW7hdbc2I"]}, "Safety Research & Evals": {"intro": "These videos show how labs and outside groups test frontier models for dangerous capabilities and keep risky systems in check, through evals, red teaming, responsible scaling policies, AI control and model organisms. Begin with how evals and scaling policies work, then go into control and the deeper research interviews.", "order": ["pO8IcIqhHuk", "E6_x0ZOXVVI", "JfDlbzF6rsY", "0pgEMWy70Qk", "JAcwtV_bFp4", "jXtk68Kzmms", "fFwg_iWWcNk", "FsGJyTfOZrs"]}, "Emergent Misalignment & Deception": {"intro": "These videos cover the experiments showing AI models can deceive, scheme, fake alignment, sandbag, or turn broadly harmful after narrow training. Start with the short demos and animated explainers, then move to the researchers' own talks and long interviews.", "order": ["xIqtVkMXc8o", "Wx6knJ1t5dk", "AqJnK9Dh-eQ", "hzlR0R91lZA", "pimIny8jJd8", "KrM5c0vp8s0", "eb2oLHblrHU", "n1Qk8xbqF-M"]}, "AI Governance & Policy": {"intro": "How governments are trying to steer AI: chip export controls and compute governance, the EU AI Act, California's SB 1047 and SB 53, and international safety reports. These picks move from the big-picture US–China race to specific laws and then to the expert debates about how far to regulate.", "order": ["k_zz3239DA0", "k_bHtx_e-SU", "oWHCyLfUgUw", "JQ8zhrsLxhI", "_0BZDUsY2do", "iCxJUDDvq94", "bLhaFRFcDVc", "ZBFG3WvweEM"]}, "AGI Timelines & the X-Risk Debate": {"intro": "When might AGI arrive, and could superintelligence threaten humanity? These videos cover the AI 2027 scenario, how forecasts shifted, and the sharp disagreements between Hinton, LeCun, Yudkowsky, Soares and Karpathy.", "order": ["k_onqn68GHY", "YeRS4TbtZWA", "giT0ytynSqg", "4__gg83s_Do", "KKN0E3a2Yzs", "TSfWqp6djck", "lXUZvyajciY", "htOvH12T7mU"]}, "AI, Jobs & the Economy": {"intro": "Is AI already taking jobs, and what could it do to growth, wages and inequality? These videos go from the latest jobs data and headline warnings to economists debating gradual change versus explosive, intelligence-explosion growth.", "order": ["8g5img1hTes", "OKCD2dmcjsQ", "HpgjB4ZA7E0", "z2OSAVwmrTs", "55BnPJlHjb8", "GT_sXIUJPUo", "WLBsUarvWTw", "Jj-kBHzUohs"]}, "How LLMs Work": {"intro": "These videos take you from what an LLM is to how transformers, attention, tokens and training actually work. Start with the short animated explainers, then go deeper with Karpathy's talks and code-along builds.", "order": ["LPZh9BOjkQs", "wjZofJX0v4M", "eMlx5fFNoYc", "zjkBMFhNj_g", "7xTGNNLPyMI", "0VLAoVGf_74", "zduSFxRajkE", "kCc8FmEb1nY"]}, "Jailbreaks, Prompt Injection & AI Security": {"intro": "This set covers how AI systems are attacked: jailbreaks, prompt injection, hijacked agents, theft of model weights and AI's own growing hacking ability. It moves from simple primers to 2026 red-team demos and expert talks on frontier security.", "order": ["jrHRe9lSqqA", "zn2ukSnDqSg", "rAEqP9VEhe8", "Sv5OLj2nVAQ", "1Hk5jvHlCzw", "zVUm23P7ZNg", "Tjw9K9mQp4I", "R63okH77k2E"]}, "AI & Society: Deepfakes, Misinformation & Privacy": {"intro": "These videos look at how AI is changing trust, elections, privacy and relationships: from deepfakes and state disinformation to facial recognition and AI companions. Together they mix practical tips, reported investigations and expert arguments.", "order": ["WSStTnoSb_c", "pg5WtBjox-Y", "X9GQiouEdJ8", "cc0dqW2HCRc", "_qtRR7f3XB8", "otAWu-bLv0Q", "_eNnUUh6-J8", "zSMcLEZgEeE"]}});
